This avoids default construction and then assignment—direct copy initialization is typically more efficient (e.g., for `std::vector`, it avoids a default-construct-then-assign cycle and goes straight to copy construction).

This is a well-known C++ optimization: prefer member initializer lists over assignment in constructors.

So the change is: switch from assignment in body to member initializer list for copy initialization, which avoids default construction of the members and directly initializes them from the source, which is faster.

